BandC <onefunus@yahoo.com> writes:
> 	About this "protection racket" strategy: Given the fact that
> some classes start with a good amount of gold, is this strategy sort
> of expected from them (to be able to survive later on)?

Not really, IMHO. The classes that start with lots of gold generally
also start with other disadvantages that make the racket more dangerous.

> Do a lot of people do this or think that it is a good strategy for
> some classes?

Does deciding, prior to starting your game, that you will pull off the
protection racket or die in the attempt make it more likely that that
one game will end is ascension? No, certainly not.

Is attempting the racket an interesting exercise that can be fun and
can hone your early-game skills? Absolutely.

Can the racket be a useful tool (in the sense of improving your ascension
chances in the current game) if the right opportunity presents itself?
Sure.

In general, though, it has an unfavorable risk-to-reward ratio. You're
taking a big risk of early death to gain a chance at ten-ish points
of AC (which you'll have enough gold to buy later anyway, even if you're
level 20+). 

> The reason I ask is: It is not cheating but I wouldn't think the
> developers had this in mind for some classes as it is kinda weird not
> to level, gather gold, and go straight to a priest you don't know
> where.

Part of the genius of nethack is that people play it in lots of ways the
developers never intended, and it still works just fine. 

